# SYNC(2)

## NAME

sync - update super-block

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int sync(void);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Sync* causes all information in core memory that should be on disk to be written out. This
includes modified super blocks, modified i-nodes, and delayed block I/O.

It should be used by programs which examine a file system, for example *icheck, df,* etc. It is
mandatory before a boot.

## SEE ALSO

sync(1), update(8)

## BUGS

The writing, although scheduled, is not necessarily complete upon return from *sync*.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_sync** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
